# Stack
- Frontend: <e.g. Next.js + TypeScript + Tailwind>
- Backend: <e.g. Node/Express + Postgres>
- Follow existing patterns in the repo before inventing new ones.

# Credit discipline
- Do the smallest change that solves the task. No unrequested refactors, renames, or "improvements".
- Never rewrite whole files. Edit only the lines that need to change.
- Don't re-read files already in context. Read only what the task needs.
- Don't run broad codebase searches when a file path is given.
- No long explanations. After a change: 1-3 lines on what changed and why.
- Don't generate docs, README updates, tests, or comments unless asked.
- If a task is ambiguous, ask ONE short question instead of guessing and rebuilding.
- If a task is large, propose a short plan (max 5 bullets) and wait for approval before coding.
- Don't retry the same failing approach twice. Stop, state the likely cause, propose a different fix.
- Don't install new dependencies without asking. Prefer what's already in package.json.

# Frontend quality
- Reuse existing components, hooks, and utils. Search for them first.
- Small, single-purpose components. Keep files under ~200 lines.
- Strict TypeScript. No `any`. Type props and API responses.
- Tailwind/design tokens only. No inline styles or magic numbers.
- Mobile-first and responsive by default.
- Every data-driven view handles loading, empty, and error states.
- Accessibility basics: semantic HTML, labels, alt text, keyboard focus, contrast.
- No unnecessary re-renders: memoize only when measured or obvious.
- Forms: validate client-side (zod or equivalent) and show clear errors.

# Backend quality
- Validate all input. Never trust the client.
- Return consistent error shapes and proper status codes.
- Secrets in env vars only. Never hardcode or log them.
- Keep route handlers thin; put logic in services.
- Parameterized queries only.

# Output format
- Show code diffs/snippets, not full files, unless creating a new file.
- Flag risks or breaking changes in one line.
